          "claim": "Let $p,q\\in\\mathbb Z$ satisfy $\\gcd(p,q)=1$. Bézout's identity gives $r,s\\in\\mathbb Z$ with $ps-qr=1$. The matrix with columns $(p,q)$ and $(r,s)$ lies in $\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb Z)$, so it induces a homeomorphism of $\\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$. The image of the first coordinate circle is embedded, essential, and represents $p[\\mu]+q[\\lambda]$. Reversing its parameter negates both coefficients, so the unoriented curve is unchanged.",
