          "quote": "Let $\\overline M$ be a compact connected oriented three-manifold whose boundary is a disjoint union of tori $T_1,\\ldots,T_k$. Choose an ordered integral basis $([\\mu_i],[\\lambda_i])$ for each $H_1(T_i;\\mathbb Z)$. A **slope** on $T_i$ is an unoriented isotopy class of essential simple closed curves whose homology class is $p_i[\\mu_i]+q_i[\\lambda_i]$ for a primitive pair $(p_i,q_i)\\in\\mathbb Z^2$; simultaneous negation gives the same unoriented slope. The primitive-pair correspondence gives exactly one such slope for each primitive pair modulo sign. The **ordinary Dehn filling** along a tuple of slopes indexed by $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$ attaches an oriented solid torus $D^2\\times S^1$ to each $T_i$, $i\\in J$, by an orientation-reversing boundary homeomorphism that sends $[\\partial D^2\\times\\{*\\}]$ to the chosen slope. The gluing maps are part of the filling data; $i\\notin J$ means that the cusp is left unfilled, denoted by the parameter $\\infty$.\n\nFor a hyperbolic deformation, the **generalized cone-filling parameter space** at each cusp is the one-point compactification $\\mathbb R^2\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ of the real coefficient plane, with $\\infty$ denoting the complete cusp. A primitive integral pair $(p_i,q_i)$ is an ordinary smooth filling coefficient. If a realized generalized parameter lies on the ray $g_i=r_i(p_i,q_i)$ with $r_i>0$ and $(p_i,q_i)$ primitive integral, its topological core direction is the ordinary slope $(p_i,q_i)$ and its cone angle is $2\\pi/r_i$. Only $r_i=1$ is the ordinary smooth filling; $r_i>1$ describes a cone or orbifold completion and does not turn the nonprimitive vector $g_i$ into a slope.",
